"An object-relational mapping diagram connects application types and members to their database tables, columns, and relationship mappings.\n\nUse when: Explain a persistence model that differs from its domain model. Review inheritance mapping and navigation-to-foreign-key behavior.\n\nAvoid when: Either the class model or schema alone answers the question.\n\nVisual grammar: Separate regions encode object types and relational tables. Mapping edges join properties to columns and navigations to key relationships. Annotations identify converters, owned types, discriminators, or inheritance strategies.\n\nBuild: Record the ORM version and relevant mapping configuration. Lay out domain types and database tables in parallel columns. Connect only nonobvious mappings and state transformation rules. Use dedicated mapping corridors so lines do not cross member lists.\n\nCheck: Each mapping matches actual configuration or is marked proposed. Navigation properties are distinguished from persisted columns. Nullability and inheritance strategy agree across both regions. Mapping anchors align with readable rows and never cover text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming one class always maps to one table. Presenting an ORM convention as a database-enforced constraint.\n\nExample: Address is an owned value object stored in Customer columns, while Order.customerId maps to a foreign key.\n\nAlternatives: Class diagram, Relational schema diagram."
